Architecture plan for issues #180-#188

Goal
- Migrate the public blog and admin flows from a Next.js + React frontend behind a Rust API proxy to a single Axum server that renders complete HTML, serves static CSS/assets, keeps the existing Postgres data model, and preserves object-storage-backed media behavior.

Target module structure
- `assets/styles/input.css`
  - Tailwind source for editorial tokens/components currently in `app/globals.css`.
  - Compiled by a standalone Tailwind CLI step into `public/assets/app.css`.
- `public/assets/`
  - Static CSS output and any non-user-uploaded public assets served by Axum through `tower_http::services::ServeDir`.
- `rust-backend/src/html/`
  - `mod.rs`: public exports and shared rendering helpers.
  - `layout.rs`: global layout context, navigation/footer data, flash/notice support, asset URL helpers.
  - `public.rs`: homepage, article, archive redirect/not-found view models and render entrypoints.
  - `admin.rs`: login, dashboard, post form/list, subscriber views and form error/notice contexts.
  - `markdown.rs`: Markdown-to-sanitized-HTML rendering, including storage-reference signing/rewrite integration.
  - `seo.rs`: page metadata, canonical URL generation, Open Graph/Twitter fields, JSON-LD builders, sitemap/robots helpers.
- `rust-backend/templates/`
  - `layout.html`, `partials/{header,footer,post_card}.html`, `public/{home,post,not_found}.html`, and admin templates added with issue #186.
  - Templates should link `/assets/app.css` and avoid JS requirements for core content or forms.
- Existing Rust modules remain the data/mutation boundary:
  - `posts`: list/detail/admin mutations, published visibility, view incrementing, media upload paths.
  - `storage`: Tigris/S3 key prefixing, signed image URLs, multipart upload support.
  - `auth`: admin credential verification/session cookies.
  - `newsletter` or equivalent handler module if split from `main.rs` during issue #185.

Shared types and boundaries
- Keep database DTOs in Rust, not templates. Route handlers should map DB rows into small template context structs before rendering.
- SEO data should be derived from the same post context used by `public::PostPage`, so titles/descriptions/canonical and JSON-LD cannot drift from visible content.
- Markdown rendering should return sanitized HTML/newtype-safe content consumed by Askama templates; do not expose raw unsanitized Markdown directly to templates.
- Storage references should continue flowing through the existing signing/proxy helpers before Markdown sanitization so `storage:` images render in server HTML without exposing raw object keys.
- Admin form handlers should reuse existing auth and mutation functions, returning redirects plus notices instead of duplicating business logic.

Issue dependency order
1. #180 Static CSS build: unblock server-rendered templates by producing `public/assets/app.css` without requiring Next/PostCSS at runtime.
2. #181 Rendering foundation: add Askama/layout/template modules and shared context conventions.
3. #182 Public Axum pages: wire `/`, `/blog`, `/blog/:slug`, 404 HTML, and `/assets` serving using existing published-post queries.
4. #183 Markdown rendering: replace React Markdown with Rust-side `pulldown-cmark` + `ammonia` + storage image signing for article content.
5. #184 SEO/sitemap/robots: depends on public HTML rendering and post contexts; replace Next sitemap/robots with Axum routes.
6. #185 Newsletter/views HTML-first: add no-JS form POST/redirect behavior and server-side view incrementing.
7. #186 Admin HTML forms: convert authenticated admin workflows after layout/form patterns are established; keep JSON endpoints only where needed for compatibility.
8. #187 Remove Next.js runtime: only after public/admin parity and tests are in place enough to validate Axum-only behavior; delete React/Next/proxy code and make the Rust binary the service entrypoint.
9. #188 Test suite/E2E: add/adjust tests throughout, then finalize coverage after the runtime migration.

Key design decisions
- Axum is the final web server for both HTML and API/form routes; avoid adding another frontend runtime once migration starts.
- Prefer progressive enhancement: homepage, article pages, newsletter signup, and admin CRUD must work through normal HTML requests and redirects without client JavaScript.
- Public origin/canonical URLs must be proxy-aware: use `X-Forwarded-Host` and `X-Forwarded-Proto` when deriving canonical, Open Graph, sitemap, redirects, or same-origin checks.
- Keep object-storage safety intact: all uploaded media keys must retain the configured storage prefix and multipart upload code must keep concrete content length handling.
- Use Askama compile-time templates for most HTML. Keep template contexts explicit and small to make tests straightforward.
- Remove Next.js only in #187, after Axum routes, CSS, templates, admin, SEO, and no-JS forms are functionally equivalent.
- Tests should exercise rendered HTML, auth redirects, configured/default login behavior, sitemap visibility, Markdown sanitization, newsletter redirects, and multipart storage invariants.
